# omp 17.3.7 `--mode rpc` HOST-TOOL round trip (agent -> host), in wire order, continuing the
# session opened by rpc-17.3.7-handshake.jsonl.
#
# Provenance: q3-probe3-hosttool-roundtrip.txt, captured by driving `omp --mode rpc` against a
# local stub model that calls the host tool `eden_ping` (omp-rpc-probe-harness.tar.gz). The host
# had registered the tool with `set_host_tools` and answered with `host_tool_result`; the whole
# exchange completed in ~2.5s with BLOCKING_UI_REQUESTS 0. VERBATIM unless marked RECONSTRUCTED.
#
# <- q3-probe3-hosttool-roundtrip.txt:10. Registering a host tool mounts it as an xd:// URI.
{"type":"notice","level":"info","message":"xd://: mounted eden_ping","source":"xdev"}
# <- q3-probe3-hosttool-roundtrip.txt:11. The set_host_tools acknowledgement, echoing the accepted
#    tool names and correlated on the host-chosen id the CAPTURED session used, "2".
#
#    THE IDS BELOW ARE ONE HIGHER THAN A CURRENT SESSION PRODUCES, and they are left that way on
#    purpose: at capture time the host spent id "1" on a negotiate_protocol it no longer sends, so
#    set_host_tools was "2" and the first prompt "3". Today rpc.go's nextFrameID hands out "1" to
#    set_host_tools and "2" to the first prompt. These are omp ECHOING ids back, and no assertion
#    correlates on them (the host-tool contract correlates on the host_tool_call's own id, further
#    down), so they are inert — but they are a capture, not a template, and rewriting the bytes to
#    match today's wire would forge a session that never ran.
{"id":"2","type":"response","command":"set_host_tools","success":true,"data":{"toolNames":["eden_ping"]}}
# <- q3-probe3-hosttool-roundtrip.txt:12. The prompt command is ACCEPTED here; the turn's
#    frames follow. The turn text rode the stdin `prompt` frame, never argv. Captured id "3"; a
#    current session numbers this one "2" (see the note above).
{"id":"3","type":"response","command":"prompt","success":true}
# <- q3-probe3-hosttool-roundtrip.txt:13-14.
{"type":"agent_start"}
{"type":"turn_start"}
# <- q3-probe3-hosttool-roundtrip.txt:23. THE correlation contract: `id` is the RPC id the host
#    must echo on host_tool_result (host-tools.ts does #pendingCalls.get(frame.id)); `toolCallId`
#    is the MODEL's tool-call id and correlates nothing on this channel. They are different
#    values on purpose, and answering with the wrong one drops the call on the floor.
{"type":"host_tool_call","id":"155cddcf9981ec56","toolCallId":"call_stub_1","toolName":"eden_ping","arguments":{"payload":"hello-eden"}}
# <- q3-probe3-hosttool-roundtrip.txt:25. Emitted AFTER the host answered; the probe proved
#    tool_execution_start is not a reliable "work has begun" signal.
{"type":"tool_execution_start","toolCallId":"call_stub_1","toolName":"eden_ping","args":{"payload":"hello-eden"}}
# <- q3-probe3-hosttool-roundtrip.txt:26. The host's result, echoed back by the agent.
{"type":"tool_execution_end","toolCallId":"call_stub_1","toolName":"eden_ping","result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"pong from eden host: {\"payload\":\"hello-eden\"}"}],"details":{}},"isError":false}
# <- q3-probe3-hosttool-roundtrip.txt:29, RECONSTRUCTED: the capture truncated the tail
#    ("…(+344B)"). The usage/cost block is completed with the values the capture already showed
#    on :22 (input 10, output 5, zero cost — the stub model is free) and toolResults is empty.
{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_stub_1","name":"eden_ping","arguments":{"payload":"hello-eden"},"streamIndex":0}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"stub","model":"stub-model","usage":{"input":10,"output":5,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":15,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop"},"toolResults":[]}
